  "text": "Let's denote the regular 24-gon by $ P_24 $. The vertices of the 24-gon are labeled $ V_1, V_2, \\ldots, V_{24} $ in order.\n\nWe need to draw 12 segments of equal lengths such that each vertex in $ P_24 $ is an endpoint of exactly one segment. This means that each vertex must be the start or end of exactly one of the 12 segments.\n\nTo solve this, we can think of the problem as assigning each vertex to one of the 12 segments. Since the segments are of equal length, the problem is essentially about finding a way to connect the vertices of the 24-gon in pairs (since each segment connects two vertices), and ensuring that each vertex is part of exactly one segment.\n\nHere's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Pairing the Vertices**: Since we have 24 vertices and 12 segments, we need to pair each vertex with another vertex such that no vertex is used more than once. This means we need to find a perfect matching in the graph formed by the 24 vertices.\n\n2. **Perfect Matching**: A perfect matching in a graph is a set of edges such that each vertex is included exactly once. In this case, we need to find a perfect matching in a complete graph with 24 vertices.\n\n3. **Number of Perfect Matchings**: The number of perfect matchings in a complete graph with $ 2n $ vertices is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   (2n-1)!! = (2n-1) \\times (2n-3) \\times (2n-5) \\times \\cdots \\times 3 \\times 1\n   \\]\n   For $ n = 12 $, we have:\n   \\[\n   23 \\times 21 \\times 19 \\times 17 \\times 15 \\times 13 \\times 11 \\times 9 \\times 7 \\times 5 \\times 3 \\times 1\n   \\]\n   This is the number of ways to pair the vertices of the 24-gon such that each vertex is part of exactly one segment.\n\n4. **Final Answer**: The number of ways to draw 12 segments of equal lengths so that each vertex in the 24-gon is an endpoint of exactly one of the 12 segments is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{23 \\times 21 \\times 19 \\times 17 \\times 15 \\times 13 \\times 11 \\times 9 \\times 7 \\times 5 \\times 3 \\times 1}\n   \\]",
